===Preposition===
{{en-head|prep}}

# [[by force of|By force of]]; [[by means of]].
#: {{ux|en|He succeeded '''by dint of''' effort, not talent.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Thomas Cooper (bishop)|Thomas Cooper]]|chapter=The Seconde Parte [of [[w:Thomas Lanquet|Lanquettes]] Chronicle]|title=Coopers Chronicle, Conteininge the Whole Discourse of the Histories as well of This Realme, as All Other Countreis,{{nb...|with the Sucession of Their Kynges, the Time of Their Raigne, and what Notable Actes were Done by Them, Newly Enlarged and Augmented, as well in the First Part, with Diuers Profitable Histories, as in the Latter Ende with the Whole Summe of Those Thinges that Paulus Iouius and Sleidane hath Written of Later Yeres, that is, from the Beginnyng of Kyng Henrie the Eightes Raigne vnto the Late Death of Queene Marie, by Me Thomas Cooper}}|edition=new|location=London|publisher={{...|[in Fletestrete,]}} &#91;[I]n the house late [[w:Thomas Berthelet|Thomas Berthelettes]]&#93;|year=1560|section=folio 27, recto|sectionurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=sSVQAQAAIAAJ&pg=RA4-PA27|oclc=1172192520|passage=[T]he Scythians perceiuyng they coulde not ouercome them '''by dint of''' ſwoorde and warre, changed their fight, & went againſt them with whippes and ſcourges, at the ſight whereof the ſeruant[s] remembring their olde condicion, were ſore abaſhed and ſubmitted them ſelues, takying worthy punyſhment for their offence.}}
#* {{RQ:Whetstone Rocke of Regard|pages=149–150|pageref=150|passage=[H]is aunceſtours did deſire / '''By dint of''' blade, not bagges of droſſe, to honour to aſpire.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Speed|chapter=Ethelred the Tvvo and Tvventieeth King of the VVest-Saxons, and the Twenty Third Monarch of the Englishmen, His Acts, and Issue|title=The Historie of Great Britaine vnder the Conqvests of the Romans, Saxons, Danes and Normans.{{nb...|Their Originals, Manners, Habits, VVarres, Coines, and Seales: With the Successions, Liues, Acts, and Issues of The English Monarchs, from Ivlivs Cæsar, to Our Most Gracious Soueraigne King Iames. The Second Edition Reuised, and Enlarged with Sundry Descents of the Saxon Kings, Their Marriages, and Armes.}}|edition=2nd|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by Iohn Beale, for}} George Hvmble,{{nb...|and are to be sold in Popes-head Pallace, at the signe of the White Horse.}}|year=1623|section=paragraph 8|page=383|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=nsw-AAAAcAAJ&pg=RA2-PA383|oclc=1050149679|passage=''[[w:Æthelred I, King of Wessex|Ethelred]]'' manfully entred the battaile, and ſo ſeconded his brother, & ouer-tyred Soldiers, that hee made way '''by dint of''' his ſword through the thickeſt of their almoſt-conquering enemies; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=William Reeves|chapter=A Preliminary Discourse to Tertullian’s Apologetick for the Christians|title=The Apologies of {{w|Justin Martyr}}, Tertullian, and [[w:Marcus Minucius Felix|Minutius Felix]], in Defence of the Christian Religion, {{...|with the Commonitory of Vincentius Lirinensis, Concerning the Primitive Rule of Faith, Translated from Their Originals; With Notes, for the Advantage Chiefly of English Readers, and a Preliminary Discourse upon each Author. Together with a Prefatory Dissertation about the Right Use of the Fathers.}} In Two Volumes|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by W. B. for}} [[w:Awnsham Churchill|A[wnsham]]] and J[ohn] Churchill,{{nb...|at the Black-Swan in Pater-Noster-Row.}}|year=1709|volume=I|page=136|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=IHpPAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA136|oclc=1076300189|passage=He {{quote-gloss|{{w|Tertullian}}}} was naturally exceeding Hot and Impatient, {{...}} to this natural Stock of Fire, and the joint Force of Letters, was added a ſurprizing Vivacity of Wit, edged with a Keeneſs peculiar to himſelf, ſo that he cou'd drive an Argument as far '''by ''dint'' of''' Reaſon, and clinch it as ſeverely '''by ''dint'' of''' Wit, as moſt Men living; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Mansfield Park|volume=III|page=66|passage=She stopt, felt herself getting into a puzzle, and could not be prevailed on to add another word, not '''by dint of''' several minutes of supplication and waiting.}}
#* {{RQ:Mill Political Economy|volume=II|chapter=Influence of Credit on Prices|section=5|page=65|passage=Other instances might be cited of parties without any capital at all, who, '''by dint of''' mere credit, were enabled, while the aspect of the market favoured their views, to make purchases to a very great extent.}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Land That Time Forgot|part=II|chapter=VIII|page=1141|column=2|passage=Nobs, '''by dint of''' much scrambling and one or two narrow escapes from death, had managed to follow us up the cliff and was now curled between me and the doorway, having devoured a piece of the dried meat, which he seemed to relish immensely.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author={{w|Eamonn Fingleton}} (witness)|chapter=Statement of Eamonn Fingleton, Economic Author: ''Unsustainable: How Economic Dogma is Destroying American American Prosperity'': Tokyo, Japan|title=China’s High Technology Development: Hearings before the [[w:United States-China Economic and Security Review Commission|U.S.–China Economic and Security Review Commission]], One Hundred Ninth [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], First Session{{nb...|April 21 and 22, 2005: Printed for the Use of the U.S.–China Economic and Security Review Commission}}|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|date=21 April 2005|page=136|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_MJeJ-rsFUoC&pg=PA136|oclc=61434017|passage='''By dint of''' learning by doing over thirty years or more, Japanese LCD manufacturers have so honed their production technologies that they can secure a yield of perfect products of as much as 90 percent in a typical production batch.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Drachinifel|title=
Anti-Slavery Patrols - The West Africa Squadron|date=1 December 2018|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TiSekII0sjw|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20241129115918/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TiSekII0sjw|archivedate=29 November 2024|accessdate=4 January 2025|time=8:00|text=This newfound power also saw more pressure being exerted, with various local chiefs and kings being pressured into signing anti-slavery treaties '''by dint of''' the Royal Navy showing up with a heavily-armed warship{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} [[because of|Because of]]; by [[reason#Noun|reason]] of.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:André Dacier|[André] Dacier]]|chapter=Verses XXI, XXII, and XXIII|translator=[anonymous]|title=The Life of {{w|Pythagoras}}, with His Symbols and {{w|Golden Verses}}.{{nb...|Together with the Life of Hierocles, and His Commentaries upon the Verses. Collected out of the Choicest Manuscripts, and Translated into French, with Annotations. By M. Dacier. Now Done in English. The Golden Verses Translated from the Greek by N[icholas] Rowe, Esq;}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} {{w|Jacob Tonson}},{{nb...|within Grays-Inn Gate next Grays-Inn Lane.}}|year=1707|section=footnote (a)|page=265|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=sIs3AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA265|oclc=1169737391|passage=[[w:Hierocles of Alexandria|''Hierocles'' [of Alexandria]]] in this Place follows the Thought of ''{{w|Socrates}}'', who in the ''[[w:Phaedo|Phædon]]'' of ''{{w|Plato}}'' deplores the hard Fate of Men, who '''by dint of''' Hearing the Ignorant diſpute and contradict every thing, imagine with themſelves that there is no ſuch thing as clear, ſolid and ſenſible Reaſons; and perſuade themſelves that every thing is uncertain.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Stephen Browne|chapter=The Future of the UN Development System|title=United Nations Development Programme and System|location=Abingdon, Oxfordshire; New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Routledge|year=2012|page=108|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hD3t6jh2UqIC&pg=PA108|isbn=978-0-415-77649-3|passage=As a development organization, it [the {{w|United Nations Development Programme}}] competes with other parts of the UN system for funds and attention, but more especially with the World Bank, which has become a substantial presence in developing countries, in terms of resources and influence. UNDP has also retained a coordination role, '''by dint of''' history and because of its broad mandate. But the two roles do not sit easily.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jonathan Healey|chapter=Preface|title=The First Century of Welfare: Poverty and Poor Relief in Lancashire, 1620–1730|location=Woodbridge, Suffolk|publisher=The Boydell Press, {{w|Boydell & Brewer}}|year=2014|page=XII|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=DhUABQAAQBAJ&pg=PR12|isbn=978-1-84383-956-9|passage=This is a book about poverty. It is a book about why some people in seventeenth and early-eighteenth-century England ended up so destitute that they called upon formal poor relief for support. {{...}} You were more likely to end up poor if you were landless, or lived away from your relatives, or simply '''by dint of''' being female.}}

